## Solution

Let $f = \frac{d\mu}{d(\mu+\nu)}$ be the Radon–Nikodym derivative. Then
$$d\mu = f\,d(\mu+\nu), \qquad d\nu = (1-f)\,d(\mu+\nu),$$
since $d(\mu+\nu) = d\mu + d\nu = f\,d(\mu+\nu) + d\nu$ gives $d\nu = (1-f)\,d(\mu+\nu)$.

**Key step.** Let $B = \{x : f(x) \ge 1\}$. On $B$ we have $1 - f \le 0$, so
$$\nu(B) = \int_B (1-f)\,d(\mu+\nu) \le 0.$$
Since $\nu$ is a (nonnegative) measure, $\nu(B) \ge 0$. Hence
$$\nu(\{x : f(x) \ge 1\}) = 0.$$

**Conclusion.** Using $\Omega = \{f < 1\} \sqcup \{f \ge 1\}$ (up to a null set for $\nu$),
$$\nu(\{x : f(x) < 1\}) = \nu(\Omega) - \nu(\{x : f(x) \ge 1\}) = 2 - 0 = \boxed{2}.$$

